  "result": "Perfect! All 150 tests pass. I've successfully added a dates module with UTC-based utilities:\n\n**src/dates.js** - Three utility functions:\n\n1. **isValidDate(text)** - Validates YYYY-MM-DD format:\n   - Checks format with regex\n   - Validates month (1-12) and day (1-31)\n   - Verifies calendar validity (Feb 30 returns false)\n   - Handles leap years correctly\n   - All operations in UTC\n\n2. **addDays(dateStr, n)** - Adds n days to a date:\n   - Accepts positive or negative days\n   - Correctly handles month/year boundaries\n   - Returns YYYY-MM-DD string\n   - Uses UTC exclusively\n\n3. **daysBetween(a, b)** - Calculates days from a to b:\n   - Returns integer number of whole days\n   - b minus a (positive if b is later)\n   - Works across month and year boundaries\n   - Uses UTC for all calculations\n\n**src/invoices.js** - Updated createInvoice:\n- Replaced inline date validation with `isValidDate(issueDate)`\n- Cleaner, more maintainable code\n- Reuses validation logic from dates module\n\n**Tests** - 20 new tests covering:\n- Valid dates in various years\n- Invalid formats (wrong separators, wrong order)\n- Invalid months (00, 13) and days (00, 32)\n- Calendar validation (Feb 30, non-leap year Feb 29)\n- Leap year support (Feb 29 in 2024 valid, in 2026 invalid)\n- addDays with positive/negative values and zero\n- Month and year boundary crossing in both directions\n- daysBetween same day, positive/negative differences\n- Large date spans\n\nAll date arithmetic is performed in UTC to ensure consistency across timezones and avoid local timezone issues.",
